{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And the son of an Israelite woman went out</b> — Its meaning is \"and he went out into the midst of the children of Israel,\" and likewise \"and he went out into the midst of the city\" (Esther 4:1), for he went out from his house or from his place of dwelling into the city, and so this one went out from his tent or from his place and came into the midst of the people, and they strove there. And the meaning of \"in the camp\" is that the quarrel took place in the camp, and many heard and seized him and brought him to Moses, to the Tent. And the reason this is mentioned here is according to the one who says he came forth from the parashah above (Leviticus Rabbah 32:3), for he had sinned with his lips concerning the fire-offerings of HaShem, and the Israelite man rebuked him, and the two of them strove, and his anger burned and he cursed under his breath. And the meaning of \"the son of the Israelite woman and the Israelite man\" is to instruct that when a gentile lies with an Israelite woman, the child is not an Israelite. And even though we have ruled in the Gemara (Yevamot 45a) that when a gentile lies with an Israelite woman the offspring is valid, whether she is unmarried or a married woman, behold they said (Bekhorot 47a): they brand the child as blemished — meaning that he is disqualified for the priesthood, and all the more so he is not an Israelite by his tribal name with regard to lineage in the banners and in the inheritance of the Land, for \"to the names of the tribes of their fathers\" is written of them (Numbers 26:55). And what is said in Tanchuma (parashah 14:1), \"in the midst of the children of Israel\" — this teaches that he converted — does not mean he required conversion per se, but rather like all Israel who entered the covenant through circumcision and immersion and the sprinkling of blood at the time of the giving of the Torah (Keritot 9a); rather, the intent is to say that he followed after his mother and attached himself to Israel. And this is the meaning of \"in the midst of the children of Israel\" — that he was among them and did not wish to follow after his father to be an Egyptian. And likewise what they said in Tanchuma (there), although there were no mamzerim at that time, he was like a mamzer — this is the opinion of an individual and is not the halakhah; the halakhah is that the offspring is valid. And the French commentators say that the reason for conversion is because this was before the giving of the Torah, and his rule was to follow after the male, from what they said (Yevamot 78a): among the nations, one follows after the male; and when this one was born they did not circumcise him, for he was an Egyptian by law, but when he grew up he converted of his own will and was circumcised. And my opinion is not so, for from the time Abraham entered the covenant, they were considered Israel, and among the nations they would not be reckoned, as they said regarding Esau (Kiddushin 18a), and perhaps an apostate Israelite is different — and all the more so: if after the giving of the Torah, when a gentile who lies with a daughter of Abraham is liable to negative commandments and has no betrothal with her, she is nonetheless a purifying mikveh for the nations to render her offspring like herself, then how much more so before the Torah, that she should purify her offspring to be like herself, to obligate him in circumcision as the seed of Abraham and to be included among the children of Israel."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:59:16.515872331Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}